The Alpha compiler selects HTML entries from authored root activity. Direct
const root inserts supply builder metadata. General `$entry` purpose validation
remains queued, and the former `$page` metadata design is superseded by builder
slots.

A template can be required to finish during compilation.

### Named const templates

A normal constant binding can hold a folded template string.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    site_name #= "Moth"

    heading #= [$md:
        # [site_name]
    ]
]

This creates a compile-time `String` value. A template assigned to a binding or
returned from a function remains a value and does not become a page fragment by itself.

### Direct top-level const fragments

Direct root const-template fragments use the current activity-based entry
selection policy. A future general `$entry` purpose is separate from metadata
and is not implemented.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$md:
        # Compile-time heading
    ]
]

The direct form on an active root:

- is valid only as top-level root fragment syntax
- must fully fold
- records its position relative to runtime fragments
- is handed to the project builder
- never becomes HIR runtime code

### Builder slot contributions

A builder can register named destinations for direct top-level const inserts.
The HTML builder registers `title`, `description`, `lang`, `favicon`,
`body_style`, `stylesheets` and `head`. Ordinary direct const fragments still
render into the body. A root with only direct named const inserts still counts
as an output-producing entry where the builder's entry rules allow it.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Moth]
    #[$in description:A language for creating reliable software]
    #[$in lang:en]
    #[$in favicon:[@assets/favicon.svg]]
    #[$in stylesheets:[@styles/site.css]]
    #[$in head:<meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff">]

    #[:<main>Hello Moth</main>]
]

The HTML destinations are:

- `title`: escaped title text, with the builder's route then project-name fallback
- `description`: escaped meta description text, omitted when absent
- `lang`: escaped `<html lang>` value, with the builder default when absent
- `favicon`: escaped icon URL from a tracked resource or a permitted String
- `body_style`: escaped body style attribute, with the builder default when absent
- `stylesheets`: one tracked local CSS resource per insert, linked in first-origin order
- `head`: raw additional head HTML, appended after the builder's dedicated head content
- the implicit default destination: ordered body fragments

A direct named insert must fold to a compile-time value. A nonconstant
contribution and a name the builder has not registered are diagnosed at their
authored source. Nested, stored and aliased inserts remain ordinary template
composition and never become builder messages. Ordinary template slots are
owned by @../templates/#template-slots (Template slots). Helper files, imported
roots and API-only roots cannot contribute to the selected entry.

The builder interprets each registered destination. Scalar metadata
contributions concatenate in source order and a supplied empty value stays
present, even for `title`. There is no scalar cardinality error. Template body
whitespace remains part of each value, including a space immediately after the
body colon. A missing value uses the builder fallback, which is separate from an
ordinary slot occurrence default. An absent title uses the route then project
name with the configured prefix and postfix. An absent description emits no
element. Other absent scalar values use the builder defaults. The builder
escapes scalar metadata and never treats it as raw HTML.

Each `stylesheets` contribution must contain exactly one tracked local CSS
resource. Plain paths, external URLs, site-root values, non-CSS files and
combined resources are invalid. Use `head` for an external stylesheet. The
builder keeps the first contribution for each stable resource origin and renders
the link relative to each output page. `head` is raw HTML emitted after the
builder's dedicated head content and deduplicated only when complete
contributions render identically in the page URL context. It never replaces or
bypasses scalar escaping.
The full HTML contract is owned by
@../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).

Named contributions keep their structural resources live, including on a page
with no runtime work. The former `page_title`, `page_description`, `page_lang`,
`page_favicon`, `page_body_style` and `page_head` names are ordinary constants
with no implicit document effect.

Runtime top-level named builder inserts are not part of the current contract.
Their final design remains unsettled. `$required`, `$unique`, loop-level
uniqueness and const function syntax are not implemented.

### Const template `if`

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[if show_heading:
        Visible heading
    ]
]

A const template `if` conditionally includes one body when its `Bool` condition
is true. It can also test an option with `is |name|`, making the captured value
available only inside the selected body. A false condition or absent option
produces structural no-output. Template `if` has no `else` or `else if` form.

The selector and body must be compile-time evaluable even when the body is not
selected. A runtime expression in an inactive body is rejected. Only a selected
body contributes output.

### Const template loops

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[loop items |item|:
        [item]
    ]
]

Const collection and range loops expand their body for each visited iteration
until the ordinary loop header ends. Template loops have no `break` or
`continue` controls. Their body must fold on every expansion.

Const loops follow the ordinary loop header contract. An explicit step must be
compile-time known. A runtime-only step is rejected with `MOTH-SYNTAX-0022`
(`invalid_template_structure.template_loop_step_not_const`). The step's sign
selects direction, a negative collection step starts at the last source item
and a collection loop's second binding is the actual source index.

The project config field `template_const_loop_iteration_limit` guards expansion:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    template_const_loop_iteration_limit
]

The default is `10_000`.

The maximum accepted configured value is `1_000_000`.

The configured value must be a positive folded `I32`. Zero, negative values and
values above `1_000_000` are rejected.

The limit counts visited iterations, not source positions. A literal zero step
is rejected by the loop header with `MOTH-SYNTAX-0029`. A step that folds to
zero is rejected with `MOTH-SYNTAX-0022`
(`invalid_template_structure.template_loop_step_is_zero`), even when the source
is empty or the range would visit nothing. A const float range step that rounds
back to the current counter is rejected with `MOTH-SYNTAX-0022`
(`invalid_template_structure.template_loop_step_no_progress`).

A const conditional loop folds to no-output only when its condition is
compile-time `false`.

A compile-time `true` condition and a runtime or unknown condition are rejected
because the compiler does not prove loop termination.

Runtime template control flow remains owned by @../templates/#template-control-flow (Template control flow).

### MON data boundary

A folded const template is an ordinary `String` that an encoder can quote. An unevaluated template is not a serialisable value. Compile time versus runtime concerns the producer and never changes document validity. The Rust codec delivers no Moth-side producer inside compiled Moth. That contract is Rust-only serialised-data documentation. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).
